    "order": "given family",
    "conventions": "Most Zambians carry an English or Christian first name and a name in their own language, and the local name is just as often used as a surname. Bemba names such as Mulenga, Chanda, Mwansa and Bwalya are both first names and family names, and many are unisex. Nyanja and Chewa names from the east carry a meaning: Chikondi (love), Madalitso (blessings), Chisomo (grace). Tonga names from the south often mark the birth: Mutinta for a girl born after boys, Choolwe for a lucky child; Tonga surnames often start with Ha- or Si-. Lozi names from the west include Mubita, Lubinda and, for women, Inonge and Namakau. English names are often virtues: Gift, Given, Bright, Precious, Mercy. Elders are addressed with the respectful prefix Ba (Ba Mulenga) or as the parent of their first child (Bashi Chanda, Bana Chanda).",
